  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Furniture and objects gathered on his world travels helped Sir George Bullough furnish Kinloch Castle. Accompanied by Robert Mitchell, he assembled a collection initially kept at Rhyddings Hall in Lancashire before moving it to Rum. The castle also housed landscapes of the island commissioned from the painter Byron Cooper, bringing local scenery into its richly furnished rooms.\n\nHorses occupied another substantial part of his life. Bullough entrusted runners to the Newmarket trainer Jack Jarvis for nearly two decades, until his death. Golden Myth won the Ascot Gold Cup in 1922, and the partnership later produced the classic-winning filly Campanula. The two men also established Longholes Stud near Newmarket.",
    "html": "<p>Furniture and objects gathered on his world travels helped Sir George Bullough furnish Kinloch Castle. Accompanied by Robert Mitchell, he assembled a collection initially kept at Rhyddings Hall in Lancashire before moving it to Rum. The castle also housed landscapes of the island commissioned from the painter Byron Cooper, bringing local scenery into its richly furnished rooms.</p>\r\n<p>Horses occupied another substantial part of his life. Bullough entrusted runners to the Newmarket trainer Jack Jarvis for nearly two decades, until his death. Golden Myth won the Ascot Gold Cup in 1922, and the partnership later produced the classic-winning filly Campanula. The two men also established Longholes Stud near Newmarket.</p>\r\n",
